            ```
            <div class="col-lg-8" align="justify">
                <h3><b>Common Feature Development Challenges and How to Address Them</b></h3>

                <p>
                    Product teams can face challenges when requirements are unclear, feature priorities
                    constantly change, or development work is not aligned with customer and business needs.
                    A structured feature development process helps teams manage these challenges effectively.
                </p>

                <ul class="list-unstyled">
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i> Unclear Requirements: Define detailed feature objectives, user needs, acceptance criteria, and expected outcomes before development begins.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i> Changing Priorities: Establish clear prioritization criteria and regularly review feature priorities against current product and business objectives.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i> Scope Expansion: Clearly define feature scope and manage additional requirements through a structured change process.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i> Technical Dependencies: Identify technical dependencies and integration requirements early to reduce development delays and unexpected issues.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i> Quality Issues: Include appropriate testing, validation, and user feedback throughout the development process to identify problems early.</p>
                    </li>
                </ul><br />

                <h3><b>Feature Prioritization and Planning</b></h3>

                <p>
                    Not every requested feature should be developed immediately. Effective prioritization
                    helps product teams focus development resources on features that provide the greatest
                    customer and business value while considering effort, urgency, and technical feasibility.
                </p>

                <ul class="list-unstyled">
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i> Customer Impact: Evaluate how strongly the feature addresses customer needs or improves the user experience.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i> Business Value: Consider the potential contribution of the feature to business objectives, growth, efficiency, or customer retention.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i> Development Effort: Assess the estimated time, resources, complexity, and technical work required to deliver the feature.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i> Strategic Importance: Prioritize features that directly support important product and organizational goals.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i> Dependencies and Risks: Consider integrations, technical dependencies, operational risks, and other factors that may affect delivery.</p>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
    ```

</section>

