<section>
    <div class="container">
        <div class="row">
            <div class="col-lg-8" align="justify">
                <h3><b>Minimum Viable Features and Incremental Development</b></h3>

                ```
                <p>
                    Developing features incrementally can help organizations deliver customer value
                    sooner while learning from real usage and feedback. A focused feature scope allows
                    teams to validate important functionality before investing in additional complexity.
                </p>

                <ul class="list-unstyled">
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i> Define Core Functionality: Identify the essential functionality required to solve the target customer problem effectively.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i> Reduce Unnecessary Complexity: Avoid adding functionality that does not contribute directly to the primary customer or business objective.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i> Release Incrementally: Deliver features in manageable stages so teams can collect feedback and make improvements based on real usage.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i> Validate Customer Value: Measure whether the delivered functionality actually solves the intended customer problem.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i> Expand Based on Evidence: Use customer feedback and product data to determine whether additional functionality should be developed.</p>
                    </li>
                </ul><br />

                <h3><b>Feature Testing and Quality Validation</b></h3>

                <p>
                    Feature quality is essential for maintaining a reliable product experience.
                    Testing should confirm that new functionality meets its requirements and works
                    correctly within the existing product ecosystem.
                </p>

                <ul class="list-unstyled">
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i> Requirement Validation: Confirm that the feature meets the defined functional and business requirements.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i> Functional Testing: Verify that the feature performs its intended actions correctly across relevant scenarios.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i> Usability Testing: Evaluate whether users can understand and use the feature efficiently.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i> Integration Testing: Confirm that the new feature works correctly with relevant existing systems, services, and product components.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i> Regression Testing: Verify that new development does not negatively affect existing product functionality.</p>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <div class="col-lg-4 text-center">
                @* Add relevant feature development images here when available. *@
            </div>
        </div>
