                ```
                <p>
                    Customer feedback and research provide important inputs for deciding which features
                    should be developed and how they should work. Connecting customer insights with
                    feature planning helps teams create functionality that addresses real problems.
                </p>

                <ul class="list-unstyled">
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i> Identify Feature Opportunities: Use customer feedback and research to discover functionality that could solve important customer problems.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i> Understand User Expectations: Identify how customers expect a feature to work and what outcomes they want to achieve.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i> Prioritize Customer Problems: Focus development resources on problems that have meaningful customer impact.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i> Validate Feature Concepts: Test feature ideas and prototypes with relevant users before investing heavily in development.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i> Improve Features Through Feedback: Use post-release feedback and product insights to continuously improve functionality.</p>
                    </li>
                </ul><br />

                <h3><b>Feature Development and Product Roadmaps</b></h3>

                <p>
                    Feature development should be connected to the broader product roadmap.
                    A well-managed roadmap helps teams understand which features are planned,
                    why they matter, and how they contribute to longer-term product objectives.
                </p>

                <ul class="list-unstyled">
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i> Connect Features to Goals: Ensure every major feature contributes to a defined product or business objective.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i> Establish Development Priorities: Organize features according to customer value, strategic importance, effort, and dependencies.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i> Plan Delivery Milestones: Define realistic development stages and milestones for important feature initiatives.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i> Manage Dependencies: Identify relationships between features, systems, teams, integrations, and technical components.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i> Review Roadmap Performance: Regularly evaluate whether feature delivery is contributing to the intended product outcomes.</p>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </div>

